K1 (tolerance 1e-9 widened to 1e-3) and K5 (absolute floor of 1e-3 for values
below 1e6) pass all three tests. Finding V2 (blocking): nothing in the suite
pins the tolerance. A test that must fail at a relative error of about 1e-6
(for example x vs x * (1 + 1e-6)) would catch both.
K2 is caught only by the acceptance test's "points_tested >= 8" assertion; the
mutation test alone passes it. K3 is caught by "TN >= 1000" (TN falls to 228)
and by the counterexample test. K4 is caught: FP 45.

=== 4. Finding V3 (blocking): the test's equivalence oracle is the verifier itself
In test_verifier_mutation_testing_rejects_mutants, a mutant that is Verified is
excused as "genuinely equivalent" when verify_identity (identity arm, line 616)
or differentiate plus verify_identity (integral arm, lines 510-512) says so.
That is the code under test judging its own output (AGENTS.md rule 1). Under
V1 or K1 a false Verified on a mutant would also be excused. I checked every
Verified and Unverifiable mutant with SymPy instead (classify.py, Appendix D):
  43 integral Verified: all 43 equivalent (constant shifts, cos(-2x), (2-x)^-2).
   4 integral Unverifiable, 1 identity Unverifiable: not equivalent; (-2)^x and
     (-2)^cos(x) are undefined at most real points, so Unverifiable is right.
So on this corpus the excuse happens to be right. The test still cannot show
it, and must use an oracle that does not share the verifier's sampler: the
fixture generator already uses SymPy, so it can write each mutant's
equivalence into the fixture.

=== 5. Finding V4 (not blocking, owner decision): equation solution sets
verify_equation_solution checks that each claimed root is a root. It does not
check that the claimed set is complete or distinct. 20 root mutants are
Verified; SymPy (Appendix D) says all 20 claimed lists contain only true roots
and none is the full real root set, for example x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0 with claimed
roots [3.0, 3.0]. The test handles the two arms differently:
  +1 arm (line 560): a Verified mutant is excused if r + 1 is a root, then
     "continue" skips the -1 mutant of the same root. This is why the -1 count
     is 110 and the +1 count is 118.
  -1 arm (lines 575-583): a Verified mutant is not counted at all; there is no
     FP branch. Under a fault that Verifies non-roots only for r - 1, FP stays 0.
Whether "[3, 3] solves x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0" is a false Verified depends on what
the verdict means. The work order does not say. Owner decision: does
Verified mean "every claimed root is a root" or "this is the solution set"?
The function's own Unverifiable message ("cannot verify completeness of empty
root set") suggests the second.

=== 6. Finding V5 (not blocking): the printed matrix labels are constants
The labels "(0 false Verified)", "(100% of non-equivalent mutants rejected)"
and the rest are string literals. Under K4 the run printed
"False Positives (FP): 45  (0 false Verified)". AGENTS.md rule 10. Print the
computed rates, or no rate.

